CET items usually combine the basic rule with an unfamiliar-looking expression, diagram, or word problem. The reliable approach is to translate the information, choose the governing relationship, solve carefully, and check the result.',objectives=array['Represent the given information accurately in plane geometry.','Select and apply the appropriate formula or property in plane geometry.','Show an efficient solution without skipping essential reasoning in plane geometry.','Check magnitude, sign, units, and reasonableness in plane geometry.']::text[],concept_sections='[{"title":"Foundation","body":"Use angle, perimeter, area, and similarity relationships."},{"title":"Governing rule","body":"Match the formula to the figure and keep units consistent."},{"title":"CET application","body":"CET items usually combine the basic rule with an unfamiliar-looking expression, diagram, or word problem. The reliable approach is to translate the information, choose the governing relationship, solve carefully, and check the result."}]'::jsonb,method_steps=array['Identify what is given and what must be found.','Rewrite the information using symbols, a table, or a labeled sketch.','Choose the property or formula that connects the known and unknown values.','Carry out one justified operation at a time and keep units consistent.','Substitute the result back or estimate to confirm that it is reasonable.']::text[],worked_examples='[{"title":"Guided example 1","passage":null,"prompt":"A rectangle is 5 cm long and 3 cm wide. What is its area in square centimeters?","answer":"15","solution":"Area = length × width = 5 × 3 = 15."},{"title":"Guided example 2","passage":null,"prompt":"A rectangle is 6 cm long and 4 cm wide. What is its perimeter in centimeters?","answer":"20","solution":"Perimeter = 2(length + width) = 2(6 + 4) = 20."},{"title":"Guided example 3","passage":null,"prompt":"A rectangle is 7 cm long and 5 cm wide. What is its area in square centimeters?","answer":"35","solution":"Area = length × width = 7 × 5 = 35."}]'::jsonb,common_mistakes=array['Starting computation before identifying the required quantity.','Applying a memorized formula to the wrong relationship.','Dropping a negative sign, unit, exponent, or grouping symbol.','Accepting an answer without checking it against the original conditions.']::text[],exam_tips=array['Estimate before solving so unreasonable options are easier to eliminate.','Write one clean intermediate line for multi-step calculations.','Use answer choices strategically, but still verify the governing method.','Return to skipped hard items after securing faster questions.']::text[],quick_checks='[{"prompt":"A rectangle is 8 cm long and 6 cm wide. What is its perimeter in centimeters?","answer":"28","explanation":"Perimeter = 2(length + width) = 2(8 + 6) = 28."},{"prompt":"A rectangle is 9 cm long and 7 cm wide. What is its area in square centimeters?","answer":"63","explanation":"Area = length × width = 9 × 7 = 63."},{"prompt":"A rectangle is 10 cm long and 8 cm wide. What is its perimeter in centimeters?","answer":"36","explanation":"Perimeter = 2(length + width) = 2(10 + 8) = 36."}]'::jsonb,source_links='[{"title":"DepEd Strengthened Senior High School Program","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/strengthened-shs-program/"},{"title":"UPCAT official site","url":"https://upcat.up.edu.ph/"},{"title":"USTET official site","url":"https://ustet.ust.edu.ph/"}]'::jsonb,estimated_minutes=20,status='published' from public.topics t join public.subjects s on s.id=t.subject_id where l.topic_id=t.id and t.slug='plane-geometry' and s.slug='mathematics';
update public.lessons l set overview='Use slope, distance, midpoint, and line equations. This lesson moves from the underlying idea to a repeatable method, then applies it to CET-style examples. The goal is not just to remember an answer but to explain why the method works and when it applies.

CET items usually combine the basic rule with an unfamiliar-looking expression, diagram, or word problem. The reliable approach is to translate the information, choose the governing relationship, solve carefully, and check the result.',objectives=array['Represent the given information accurately in coordinate geometry.','Select and apply the appropriate formula or property in coordinate geometry.','Show an efficient solution without skipping essential reasoning in coordinate geometry.','Check magnitude, sign, units, and reasonableness in coordinate geometry.']::text[],concept_sections='[{"title":"Foundation","body":"Use slope, distance, midpoint, and line equations."},{"title":"Governing rule","body":"Slope is change in y divided by change in x."},{"title":"CET application","body":"CET items usually combine the basic rule with an unfamiliar-looking expression, diagram, or word problem. The reliable approach is to translate the information, choose the governing relationship, solve carefully, and check the result."}]'::jsonb,method_steps=array['Identify what is given and what must be found.','Rewrite the information using symbols, a table, or a labeled sketch.','Choose the property or formula that connects the known and unknown values.','Carry out one justified operation at a time and keep units consistent.','Substitute the result back or estimate to confirm that it is reasonable.']::text[],worked_examples='[{"title":"Guided example 1","passage":null,"prompt":"What is the slope of the line through (0, 1) and (2, 3)?","answer":"1","solution":"Slope = (3 - 1)/(2 - 0) = 2/2 = 1."},{"title":"Guided example 2","passage":null,"prompt":"What is the slope of the line through (1, 3) and (4, 9)?","answer":"2","solution":"Slope = (9 - 3)/(4 - 1) = 6/3 = 2."},{"title":"Guided example 3","passage":null,"prompt":"What is the slope of the line through (2, 5) and (6, 17)?","answer":"3","solution":"Slope = (17 - 5)/(6 - 2) = 12/4 = 3."}]'::jsonb,common_mistakes=array['Starting computation before identifying the required quantity.','Applying a memorized formula to the wrong relationship.','Dropping a negative sign, unit, exponent, or grouping symbol.','Accepting an answer without checking it against the original conditions.']::text[],exam_tips=array['Estimate before solving so unreasonable options are easier to eliminate.','Write one clean intermediate line for multi-step calculations.','Use answer choices strategically, but still verify the governing method.','Return to skipped hard items after securing faster questions.']::text[],quick_checks='[{"prompt":"What is the slope of the line through (3, 7) and (8, 27)?","answer":"4","explanation":"Slope = (27 - 7)/(8 - 3) = 20/5 = 4."},{"prompt":"What is the slope of the line through (4, 9) and (6, 19)?","answer":"5","explanation":"Slope = (19 - 9)/(6 - 4) = 10/2 = 5."},{"prompt":"What is the slope of the line through (5, 11) and (8, 14)?","answer":"1","explanation":"Slope = (14 - 11)/(8 - 5) = 3/3 = 1."}]'::jsonb,source_links='[{"title":"DepEd Strengthened Senior High School Program","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/strengthened-shs-program/"},{"title":"UPCAT official site","url":"https://upcat.up.edu.ph/"},{"title":"USTET official site","url":"https://ustet.ust.edu.ph/"}]'::jsonb,estimated_minutes=20,status='published' from public.topics t join public.subjects s on s.id=t.subject_id where l.topic_id=t.id and t.slug='coordinate-geometry' and s.slug='mathematics';
update public.lessons l set overview='Relate angles and side lengths in right triangles. This lesson moves from the underlying idea to a repeatable method, then applies it to CET-style examples. The goal is not just to remember an answer but to explain why the method works and when it applies.

