 not eliminate all flood risk.","prompt":"Which conclusion best combines the details in the passage?","answer":"Mangrove restoration can reduce erosion but is not complete flood protection.","solution":"The answer stays within the evidence: Mangrove restoration can reduce erosion but is not complete flood protection."},{"title":"Guided example 3","passage":"A school replaced disposable cups with refill stations. Plastic waste fell, but water use rose slightly because the stations required daily cleaning.","prompt":"Which conclusion best combines the details in the passage?","answer":"The refill program reduced plastic while creating a smaller water-use tradeoff.","solution":"The answer stays within the evidence: The refill program reduced plastic while creating a smaller water-use tradeoff."}]'::jsonb,common_mistakes=array['Selecting a true statement that does not answer the question.','Using personal knowledge instead of passage evidence.','Treating one detail as the main idea of the entire passage.','Choosing extreme language when the author uses qualified language.']::text[],exam_tips=array['Return to the relevant lines instead of relying on memory.','Watch contrast signals such as however, although, and but.','For inference, choose what must or most reasonably follows.','Check axes, units, legends, and time periods in visual information.']::text[],quick_checks='[{"prompt":"Which conclusion best combines the details in the passage?","answer":"The better variety depends on the expected water conditions.","explanation":"The answer stays within the evidence: The better variety depends on the expected water conditions."},{"prompt":"Which conclusion best combines the details in the passage?","answer":"Protected lanes increased cycling without substantially delaying buses.","explanation":"The answer stays within the evidence: Protected lanes increased cycling without substantially delaying buses."},{"prompt":"Which conclusion best combines the details in the passage?","answer":"Spaced review improved recall in the described comparison.","explanation":"The answer stays within the evidence: Spaced review improved recall in the described comparison."}]'::jsonb,source_links='[{"title":"DepEd Strengthened Senior High School Program","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/strengthened-shs-program/"},{"title":"UPCAT official site","url":"https://upcat.up.edu.ph/"},{"title":"USTET official site","url":"https://ustet.ust.edu.ph/"}]'::jsonb,estimated_minutes=20,status='published' from public.topics t join public.subjects s on s.id=t.subject_id where l.topic_id=t.id and t.slug='synthesis' and s.slug='reading-comprehension';
update public.lessons l set overview='Read tables, charts, captions, and text together. This lesson moves from the underlying idea to a repeatable method, then applies it to CET-style examples. The goal is not just to remember an answer but to explain why the method works and when it applies.

Reading questions are evidence tasks. A defensible answer must match the passage’s actual scope, wording, and purpose—not merely something that could be true outside the text.',objectives=array['State the central idea at the correct level of scope in visual and quantitative information.','Locate evidence for explicit and inferred claims in visual and quantitative information.','Analyze tone, purpose, structure, and context in visual and quantitative information.','Synthesize prose with tables or a second text in visual and quantitative information.']::text[],concept_sections='[{"title":"Foundation","body":"Read tables, charts, captions, and text together."},{"title":"Governing rule","body":"Check units, labels, scale, and comparison basis."},{"title":"CET application","body":"Reading questions are evidence tasks. A defensible answer must match the passage’s actual scope, wording, and purpose—not merely something that could be true outside the text."}]'::jsonb,method_steps=array['Preview the question so you know what evidence to track.','Read the passage for its claim, structure, and changes in direction.','Mark the sentence or data point that supports each possible answer.','Reject choices that are unsupported, too broad, too narrow, or reversed.','Restate the winning answer in your own words and compare it with the text.']::text[],worked_examples='[{"title":"Guided example 1","passage":"A barangay library extended its hours during examination week. Attendance rose most on evenings when volunteer tutors were present. The librarian plans to keep two evening schedules each week.","prompt":"If the passage were paired with a chart, which measure would be most relevant?","answer":"the before-and-after value of the reported outcome","solution":"The answer stays within the evidence: Evening access paired with tutoring increased library use."},{"title":"Guided example 2","passage":"Mangrove roots slow coastal water and trap sediment. Communities that restored mangroves recorded less erosion during ordinary storms, though the trees did not eliminate all flood risk.","prompt":"If the passage were paired with a chart, which measure would be most relevant?","answer":"the before-and-after value of the reported outcome","solution":"The answer stays within the evidence: Mangrove restoration can reduce erosion but is not complete flood protection."},{"title":"Guided example 3","passage":"A school replaced disposable cups with refill stations. Plastic waste fell, but water use rose slightly because the stations required daily cleaning.","prompt":"If the passage were paired with a chart, which measure would be most relevant?","answer":"the before-and-after value of the reported outcome","solution":"The answer stays within the evidence: The refill program reduced plastic while creating a smaller water-use tradeoff."}]'::jsonb,common_mistakes=array['Selecting a true statement that does not answer the question.','Using personal knowledge instead of passage evidence.','Treating one detail as the main idea of the entire passage.','Choosing extreme language when the author uses qualified language.']::text[],exam_tips=array['Return to the relevant lines instead of relying on memory.','Watch contrast signals such as however, although, and but.','For inference, choose what must or most reasonably follows.','Check axes, units, legends, and time periods in visual information.']::text[],quick_checks='[{"prompt":"If the passage were paired with a chart, which measure would be most relevant?","answer":"the before-and-after value of the reported outcome","explanation":"The answer stays within the evidence: The better variety depends on the expected water conditions."},{"prompt":"If the passage were paired with a chart, which measure would be most relevant?","answer":"the before-and-after value of the reported outcome","explanation":"The answer stays within the evidence: Protected lanes increased cycling without substantially delaying buses."},{"prompt":"If the passage were paired with a chart, which measure would be most relevant?","answer":"the before-and-after value of the reported outcome","explanation":"The answer stays within the evidence: Spaced review improved recall in the described comparison."}]'::jsonb,source_links='[{"title":"DepEd Strengthened Senior High School Program","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/strengthened-shs-program/"},{"title":"UPCAT official site","url":"https://upcat.up.edu.ph/"},{"title":"USTET official site","url":"https://ustet.ust.edu.ph/"}]'::jsonb,estimated_minutes=20,status='published' from public.topics t join public.subjects s on s.id=t.subject_id where l.topic_id=t.id and t.slug='visual-information' and s.slug='reading-comprehension';
update public.lessons l set overview='Group items by a consistent defining property. This lesson moves from the underlying idea to a repeatable method, then applies it to CET-style examples. The goal is not just to remember an answer but to explain why the method works and when it applies.

Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element.',objectives=array['Identify a consistent relationship or pattern in classification and odd-one-out.','Distinguish necessary conclusions from possible ones in classification and odd-one-out.','Evaluate assumptions and evidence in classification and odd-one-out.','Judge whether information is sufficient in classification and odd-one-out.']::text[],concept_sections='[{"title":"Foundation","body":"Group items by a consistent defining property."},{"title":"Governing rule","body":"Name the rule that includes all but one item."},{"title":"CET application","body":"Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element."}]'::jsonb,method_steps=array['List only the facts, positions, or symbols actually given.','Test simple features first: count, difference, ratio, order, direction, or category.','State the candidate rule explicitly.','Apply it to every element and reject it if even one element conflicts.','Use the surviving rule to derive the answer without adding unstated facts.']::text[],worked_examples='[{"title":"Guided example 1","passage":null,"prompt":"Which item does not belong with triangle, square, pentagon?","answer":"circle","solution":"triangle, square, pentagon are polygons with straight sides; circle is not."},{"title":"Guided example 2","passage":null,"prompt":"Which item does not belong with mango, banana, papaya?","answer":"carrot","solution":"mango, banana, papaya are fruits; carrot is not."},{"title":"Guided example 3","passage":null,"prompt":"Which item does not belong with copper, iron, gold?","answer":"glass","solution":"copper, iron, gold are metals; glass is not."}]'::jsonb,common_mistakes=array['Choosing a pattern that fits only the last two terms.','Reversing the direction of an analogy.','Assuming the converse of a conditional statement.','Combining statements before testing each one separately in data sufficiency.']::text[],exam_tips=array['Convert letters to positions and diagrams to simple feature lists.','For syllogisms, draw sets or use a small Venn diagram.','In arguments, ask which fact directly changes confidence in the conclusion.','Move on when no stable rule appears, then revisit with fresh attention.']::text[],quick_checks='[{"prompt":"Which item does not belong with sparrow, eagle, owl?","answer":"bat","explanation":"sparrow, eagle, owl are birds; bat is not."},{"prompt":"Which item does not belong with Mercury, Venus, Mars?","answer":"Moon","explanation":"Mercury, Venus, Mars are planets; Moon is not."},{"prompt":"Which item does not belong with meter, kilometer, centimeter?","answer":"liter","explanation":"meter, kilometer, centimeter are units of length; liter is not."}]'::jsonb,source_links='[{"title":"DepEd Strengthened Senior High School Program","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/strengthened-shs-program/"},{"title":"UPCAT official site","url":"https://upcat.up.edu.ph/"},{"title":"USTET official site","url":"https://ustet.ust.edu.ph/"}]'::jsonb,estimated_minutes=20,status='published' from public.topics t join public.subjects s on s.id=t.subject_id where l.topic_id=t.id and t.slug='classification' and s.slug='mental-logical-reasoning';
update public.lessons l set overview='Detect arithmetic, geometric, and alternating rules. This lesson moves from the underlying idea to a repeatable method, then applies it to CET-style examples. The goal is not just to remember an answer but to explain why the method works and when it applies.

Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element.',objectives=array['Identify a consistent relationship or pattern in number series.','Distinguish necessary conclusions from possible ones in number series.','Evaluate assumptions and evidence in number series.','Judge whether information is sufficient in number series.']::text[],concept_sections='[{"title":"Foundation","body":"Detect arithmetic, geometric, and alternating rules."},{"title":"Governing rule","body":"Test simple differences and ratios before complex patterns."},{"title":"CET application","body":"Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element."}]'::jsonb,method_steps=array['List only the facts, positions, or symbols actually given.','Test simple features first: count, difference, ratio, order, direction, or category.','State the candidate rule explicitly.','Apply it to every element and reject it if even one element conflicts.','Use the surviving rule to derive the answer without adding unstated facts.']::text[],worked_examples='[{"title":"Guided example 1","passage":null,"prompt":"What comes next: 2, 4, 6, 8, ___?","answer":"10","solution":"Each term increases by 2, so the next term is 10."},{"title":"Guided example 2","passage":null,"prompt":"What comes next: 3, 6, 9, 12, ___?","answer":"15","solution":"Each term increases by 3, so the next term is 15."},{"title":"Guided example 3","passage":null,"prompt":"What comes next: 4, 8, 12, 16, ___?","answer":"20","solution":"Each term increases by 4, so the next term is 20."}]'::jsonb,common_mistakes=array['Choosing a pattern that fits only the last two terms.','Reversing the direction of an analogy.','Assuming the converse of a conditional statement.','Combining statements before testing each one separately in data sufficiency.']::text[],exam_tips=array['Convert letters to positions and diagrams to simple feature lists.','For syllogisms, draw sets or use a small Venn diagram.','In arguments, ask which fact directly changes confidence in the conclusion.','Move on when no stable rule appears, then revisit with fresh attention.']::text[],quick_checks='[{"prompt":"What comes next: 5, 10, 15, 20, ___?","answer":"25","explanation":"Each term increases by 5, so the next term is 25."},{"prompt":"What comes next: 6, 12, 18, 24, ___?","answer":"30","explanation":"Each term increases by 6, so the next term is 30."},{"prompt":"What comes next: 7, 9, 11, 13, ___?","answer":"15","explanation":"Each term increases by 2, so the next term is 15."}]'::jsonb,source_links='[{"title":"DepEd Strengthened Senior High School Program","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/strengthened-shs-program/"},{"title":"UPCAT official site","url":"https://upcat.up.edu.ph/"},{"title":"USTET official site","url":"https://ustet.ust.edu.ph/"}]'::jsonb,estimated_minutes=20,status='published' from public.topics t join public.subjects s on s.id=t.subject_id where l.topic_id=t.id and t.slug='number-series' and s.slug='mental-logical-reasoning';
update public.lessons l set overview='Track positions, cycles, and alternating symbol rules. This lesson moves from the underlying idea to a repeatable method, then applies it to CET-style examples. The goal is not just to remember an answer but to explain why the method works and when it applies.

Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element.',objectives=array['Identify a consistent relationship or pattern in letter and symbol series.','Distinguish necessary conclusions from possible ones in letter and symbol series.','Evaluate assumptions and evidence in letter and symbol series.','Judge whether information is sufficient in letter and symbol series.']::text[],concept_sections='[{"title":"Foundation","body":"Track positions, cycles, and alternating symbol rules."},{"title":"Governing rule","body":"Convert letters to alphabet positions when useful."},{"title":"CET application","body":"Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element."}]'::jsonb,method_steps=array['List only the facts, positions, or symbols actually given.','Test simple features first: count, difference, ratio, order, direction, or category.','State the candidate rule explicitly.','Apply it to every element and reject it if even one element conflicts.','Use the surviving rule to derive the answer without adding unstated facts.']::text[],worked_examples='[{"title":"Guided example 1","passage":null,"prompt":"What comes next: A, B, C, D, ___?","answer":"E","solution":"The pattern advances 1 alphabet position each time."},{"title":"Guided example 2","passage":null,"prompt":"What comes next: B, C, D, E, ___?","answer":"F","solution":"The pattern advances 1 alphabet position each time."},{"title":"Guided example 3","passage":null,"prompt":"What comes next: C, D, E, F, ___?","answer":"G","solution":"The pattern advances 1 alphabet position each time."}]'::jsonb,common_mistakes=array['Choosing a pattern that fits only the last two terms.','Reversing the direction of an analogy.','Assuming the converse of a conditional statement.','Combining statements before testing each one separately in data sufficiency.']::text[],exam_tips=array['Convert letters to positions and diagrams to simple feature lists.','For syllogisms, draw sets or use a small Venn diagram.','In arguments, ask which fact directly changes confidence in the conclusion.','Move on when no stable rule appears, then revisit with fresh attention.']::text[],quick_checks='[{"prompt":"What comes next: D, E, F, G, ___?","answer":"H","explanation":"The pattern advances 1 alphabet position each time."},{"prompt":"What comes next: E, F, G, H, ___?","answer":"I","explanation":"The pattern advances 1 alphabet position each time."},{"prompt":"What comes next: F, G, H, I, ___?","answer":"J","explanation":"The pattern advances 1 alphabet position each time."}]'::jsonb,source_links='[{"title":"DepEd Strengthened Senior High School Program","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/strengthened-shs-program/"},{"title":"UPCAT official site","url":"https://upcat.up.edu.ph/"},{"title":"USTET official site","url":"https://ustet.ust.edu.ph/"}]'::jsonb,estimated_minutes=20,status='published' from public.topics t join public.subjects s on s.id=t.subject_id where l.topic_id=t.id and t.slug='letter-series' and s.slug='mental-logical-reasoning';
update public.lessons l set overview='Identify changes in shape, count, orientation, and shading. This lesson moves from the underlying idea to a repeatable method, then applies it to CET-style examples. The goal is not just to remember an answer but to explain why the method works and when it applies.

Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element.',objectives=array['Identify a consistent relationship or pattern in abstract patterns.','Distinguish necessary conclusions from possible ones in abstract patterns.','Evaluate assumptions and evidence in abstract patterns.','Judge whether information is sufficient in abstract patterns.']::text[],concept_sections='[{"title":"Foundation","body":"Identify changes in shape, count, orientation, and shading."},{"title":"Governing rule","body":"Track one visual feature at a time."},{"title":"CET application","body":"Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element."}]'::jsonb,method_steps=array['List only the facts, positions, or symbols actually given.','Test simple features first: count, difference, ratio, order, direction, or category.','State the candidate rule explicitly.','Apply it to every element and reject it if even one element conflicts.','Use the surviving rule to derive the answer without adding unstated facts.']::text[],worked_examples='[{"title":"Guided example 1","passage":null,"prompt":"A marker rotates 45° clockwise per step. What is its net clockwise rotation after 2 steps?","answer":"90°","solution":"45° × 2 = 90°; removing full 360° turns gives 90°."},{"title":"Guided example 2","passage":null,"prompt":"A marker rotates 60° clockwise per step. What is its net clockwise rotation after 2 steps?","answer":"120°","solution":"60° × 2 = 120°; removing full 360° turns gives 120°."},{"title":"Guided example 3","passage":null,"prompt":"A marker rotates 90° clockwise per step. What is its net clockwise rotation after 2 steps?","answer":"180°","solution":"90° × 2 = 180°; removing full 360° turns gives 180°."}]'::jsonb,common_mistakes=array['Choosing a pattern that fits only the last two terms.','Reversing the direction of an analogy.','Assuming the converse of a conditional statement.','Combining statements before testing each one separately in data sufficiency.']::text[],exam_tips=array['Convert letters to positions and diagrams to simple feature lists.','For syllogisms, draw sets or use a small Venn diagram.','In arguments, ask which fact directly changes confidence in the conclusion.','Move on when no stable rule appears, then revisit with fresh attention.']::text[],quick_checks='[{"prompt":"A marker rotates 120° clockwise per step. What is its net clockwise rotation after 2 steps?","answer":"240°","explanation":"120° × 2 = 240°; removing full 360° turns gives 240°."},{"prompt":"A marker rotates 45° clockwise per step. What is its net clockwise rotation after 3 steps?","answer":"135°","explanation":"45° × 3 = 135°; removing full 360° turns gives 135°."},{"prompt":"A marker rotates 60° clockwise per step. What is its net clockwise rotation after 3 steps?","answer":"180°","explanation":"60° × 3 = 180°; removing full 360° turns gives 180°."}]'::jsonb,source_links='[{"title":"DepEd Strengthened Senior High School Program","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/strengthened-shs-program/"},{"title":"UPCAT official site","url":"https://upcat.up.edu.ph/"},{"title":"USTET official site","url":"https://ustet.ust.edu.ph/"}]'::jsonb,estimated_minutes=20,status='published' from public.topics t join public.subjects s on s.id=t.subject_id where l.topic_id=t.id and t.slug='abstract-patterns' and s.slug='mental-logical-reasoning';
update public.lessons l set overview='Reason about rotations, reflections, folds, and views. This lesson moves from the underlying idea to a repeatable method, then applies it to CET-style examples. The goal is not just to remember an answer but to explain why the method works and when it applies.

Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element.',objectives=array['Identify a consistent relationship or pattern in spatial relationships.','Distinguish necessary conclusions from possible ones in spatial relationships.','Evaluate assumptions and evidence in spatial relationships.','Judge whether information is sufficient in spatial relationships.']::text[],concept_sections='[{"title":"Foundation","body":"Reason about rotations, reflections, folds, and views."},{"title":"Governing rule","body":"A rotation preserves shape and size; a reflection reverses orientation."},{"title":"CET application","body":"Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element."}]'::jsonb,method_steps=array['List only the facts, positions, or symbols actually given.','Test simple features first: count, difference, ratio, order, direction, or category.','State the candidate rule explicitly.','Apply it to every element and reject it if even one element conflicts.','Use the surviving rule to derive the answer without adding unstated facts.']::text[],worked_examples='[{"title":"Guided example 1","passage":null,"prompt":"An arrow points north. It makes 1 quarter-turn clockwise. Which direction does it point?","answer":"east","solution":"Moving 1 quarter-turn clockwise from north ends at east."},{"title":"Guided example 2","passage":null,"prompt":"An arrow points east. It makes 1 quarter-turn clockwise. Which direction does it point?","answer":"south","solution":"Moving 1 quarter-turn clockwise from east ends at south."},{"title":"Guided example 3","passage":null,"prompt":"An arrow points south. It makes 1 quarter-turn clockwise. Which direction does it point?","answer":"west","solution":"Moving 1 quarter-turn clockwise from south ends at west."}]'::jsonb,common_mistakes=array['Choosing a pattern that fits only the last two terms.','Reversing the direction of an analogy.','Assuming the converse of a conditional statement.','Combining statements before testing each one separately in data sufficiency.']::text[],exam_tips=array['Convert letters to positions and diagrams to simple feature lists.','For syllogisms, draw sets or use a small Venn diagram.','In arguments, ask which fact directly changes confidence in the conclusion.','Move on when no stable rule appears, then revisit with fresh attention.']::text[],quick_checks='[{"prompt":"An arrow points west. It makes 1 quarter-turn clockwise. Which direction does it point?","answer":"north","explanation":"Moving 1 quarter-turn clockwise from west ends at north."},{"prompt":"An arrow points north. It makes 2 quarter-turns clockwise. Which direction does it point?","answer":"south","explanation":"Moving 2 quarter-turns clockwise from north ends at south."},{"prompt":"An arrow points east. It makes 2 quarter-turns clockwise. Which direction does it point?","answer":"west","explanation":"Moving 2 quarter-turns clockwise from east ends at west."}]'::jsonb,source_links='[{"title":"DepEd Strengthened Senior High School Program","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/strengthened-shs-program/"},{"title":"UPCAT official site","url":"https://upcat.up.edu.ph/"},{"title":"USTET official site","url":"https://ustet.ust.edu.ph/"}]'::jsonb,estimated_minutes=20,status='published' from public.topics t join public.subjects s on s.id=t.subject_id where l.topic_id=t.id and t.slug='spatial-reasoning' and s.slug='mental-logical-reasoning';
update public.lessons l set overview='Transfer a precise relationship to a new pair. This lesson moves from the underlying idea to a repeatable method, then applies it to CET-style examples. The goal is not just to remember an answer but to explain why the method works and when it applies.

Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element.',objectives=array['Identify a consistent relationship or pattern in logical analogies.','Distinguish necessary conclusions from possible ones in logical analogies.','Evaluate assumptions and evidence in logical analogies.','Judge whether information is sufficient in logical analogies.']::text[],concept_sections='[{"title":"Foundation","body":"Transfer a precise relationship to a new pair."},{"title":"Governing rule","body":"The relationship must work in the same direction."},{"title":"CET application","body":"Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element."}]'::jsonb,method_steps=array['List only the facts, positions, or symbols actually given.','Test simple features first: count, difference, ratio, order, direction, or category.','State the candidate rule explicitly.','Apply it to every element and reject it if even one element conflicts.','Use the surviving rule to derive the answer without adding unstated facts.']::text[],worked_examples='[{"title":"Guided example 1","passage":null,"prompt":"puppy is to dog as kitten is to _____.","answer":"cat","solution":"The relationship from puppy to dog matches the relationship from kitten to cat."},{"title":"Guided example 2","passage":null,"prompt":"calf is to cow as foal is to _____.","answer":"horse","solution":"The relationship from calf to cow matches the relationship from foal to horse."},{"title":"Guided example 3","passage":null,"prompt":"page is to book as brick is to _____.","answer":"wall","solution":"The relationship from page to book matches the relationship from brick to wall."}]'::jsonb,common_mistakes=array['Choosing a pattern that fits only the last two terms.','Reversing the direction of an analogy.','Assuming the converse of a conditional statement.','Combining statements before testing each one separately in data sufficiency.']::text[],exam_tips=array['Convert letters to positions and diagrams to simple feature lists.','For syllogisms, draw sets or use a small Venn diagram.','In arguments, ask which fact directly changes confidence in the conclusion.','Move on when no stable rule appears, then revisit with fresh attention.']::text[],quick_checks='[{"prompt":"finger is to hand as toe is to _____.","answer":"foot","explanation":"The relationship from finger to hand matches the relationship from toe to foot."},{"prompt":"captain is to ship as pilot is to _____.","answer":"airplane","explanation":"The relationship from captain to ship matches the relationship from pilot to airplane."},{"prompt":"thermometer is to temperature as scale is to _____.","answer":"weight","explanation":"The relationship from thermometer to temperature matches the relationship from scale to weight."}]'::jsonb,source_links='[{"title":"DepEd Strengthened Senior High School Program","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/strengthened-shs-program/"},{"title":"UPCAT official site","url":"https://upcat.up.edu.ph/"},{"title":"USTET official site","url":"https://ustet.ust.edu.ph/"}]'::jsonb,estimated_minutes=20,status='published' from public.topics t join public.subjects s on s.id=t.subject_id where l.topic_id=t.id and t.slug='logical-analogies' and s.slug='mental-logical-reasoning';
update public.lessons l set overview='Decide what must follow from stated premises. This lesson moves from the underlying idea to a repeatable method, then applies it to CET-style examples. The goal is not just to remember an answer but to explain why the method works and when it applies.

Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element.',objectives=array['Identify a consistent relationship or pattern in syllogisms and deductions.','Distinguish necessary conclusions from possible ones in syllogisms and deductions.','Evaluate assumptions and evidence in syllogisms and deductions.','Judge whether information is sufficient in syllogisms and deductions.']::text[],concept_sections='[{"title":"Foundation","body":"Decide what must follow from stated premises."},{"title":"Governing rule","body":"Do not add facts that the premises do not supply."},{"title":"CET application","body":"Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element."}]'::jsonb,method_steps=array['List only the facts, positions, or symbols actually given.','Test simple features first: count, difference, ratio, order, direction, or category.','State the candidate rule explicitly.','Apply it to every element and reject it if even one element conflicts.','Use the surviving rule to derive the answer without adding unstated facts.']::text[],worked_examples='[{"title":"Guided example 1","passage":null,"prompt":"All scholars belong to readers. Mira belongs to scholars. What must follow?","answer":"Mira belongs to readers.","solution":"The premises place every scholar inside the readers group, including Mira."},{"title":"Guided example 2","passage":null,"prompt":"All orchids belong to plants. Lina belongs to orchids. What must follow?","answer":"Lina belongs to plants.","solution":"The premises place every orchid inside the plants group, including Lina."},{"title":"Guided example 3","passage":null,"prompt":"All triangles belong to polygons. figure Q belongs to triangles. What must follow?","answer":"figure Q belongs to polygons.","solution":"The premises place every triangle inside the polygons group, including figure Q."}]'::jsonb,common_mistakes=array['Choosing a pattern that fits only the last two terms.','Reversing the direction of an analogy.','Assuming the converse of a conditional statement.','Combining statements before testing each one separately in data sufficiency.']::text[],exam_tips=array['Convert letters to positions and diagrams to simple feature lists.','For syllogisms, draw sets or use a small Venn diagram.','In arguments, ask which fact directly changes confidence in the conclusion.','Move on when no stable rule appears, then revisit with fresh attention.']::text[],quick_checks='[{"prompt":"All whales belong to mammals. animal W belongs to whales. What must follow?","answer":"animal W belongs to mammals.","explanation":"The premises place every whale inside the mammals group, including animal W."},{"prompt":"All nurses belong to health workers. Paolo belongs to nurses. What must follow?","answer":"Paolo belongs to health workers.","explanation":"The premises place every nurse inside the health workers group, including Paolo."},{"prompt":"All novels belong to books. text N belongs to novels. What must follow?","answer":"text N belongs to books.","explanation":"The premises place every novel inside the books group, including text N."}]'::jsonb,source_links='[{"title":"DepEd Strengthened Senior High School Program","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/strengthened-shs-program/"},{"title":"UPCAT official site","url":"https://upcat.up.edu.ph/"},{"title":"USTET official site","url":"https://ustet.ust.edu.ph/"}]'::jsonb,estimated_minutes=20,status='published' from public.topics t join public.subjects s on s.id=t.subject_id where l.topic_id=t.id and t.slug='syllogisms' and s.slug='mental-logical-reasoning';
update public.lessons l set overview='Identify hidden premises and evaluate evidence. This lesson moves from the underlying idea to a repeatable method, then applies it to CET-style examples. The goal is not just to remember an answer but to explain why the method works and when it applies.

Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element.',objectives=array['Identify a consistent relationship or pattern in assumptions and arguments.','Distinguish necessary conclusions from possible ones in assumptions and arguments.','Evaluate assumptions and evidence in assumptions and arguments.','Judge whether information is sufficient in assumptions and arguments.']::text[],concept_sections='[{"title":"Foundation","body":"Identify hidden premises and evaluate evidence."},{"title":"Governing rule","body":"A necessary assumption must hold for the conclusion to follow."},{"title":"CET application","body":"Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element."}]'::jsonb,method_steps=array['List only the facts, positions, or symbols actually given.','Test simple features first: count, difference, ratio, order, direction, or category.','State the candidate rule explicitly.','Apply it to every element and reject it if even one element conflicts.','Use the surviving rule to derive the answer without adding unstated facts.']::text[],worked_examples='[{"title":"Guided example 1","passage":null,"prompt":"A school claims its new schedule improved punctuality. Which evidence most strengthens the claim?","answer":"late arrivals improved after the new schedule, while the comparison conditions stayed stable.","solution":"The correct choice directly measures punctuality and includes a useful comparison."},{"title":"Guided example 2","passage":null,"prompt":"A school claims its tutoring program improved algebra performance. Which evidence most strengthens the claim?","answer":"algebra scores improved after the tutoring program, while the comparison conditions stayed stable.","solution":"The correct choice directly measures algebra performance and includes a useful comparison."},{"title":"Guided example 3","passage":null,"prompt":"A school claims its bike lane improved cycling participation. Which evidence most strengthens the claim?","answer":"bicycle counts improved after the bike lane, while the comparison conditions stayed stable.","solution":"The correct choice directly measures cycling participation and includes a useful comparison."}]'::jsonb,common_mistakes=array['Choosing a pattern that fits only the last two terms.','Reversing the direction of an analogy.','Assuming the converse of a conditional statement.','Combining statements before testing each one separately in data sufficiency.']::text[],exam_tips=array['Convert letters to positions and diagrams to simple feature lists.','For syllogisms, draw sets or use a small Venn diagram.','In arguments, ask which fact directly changes confidence in the conclusion.','Move on when no stable rule appears, then revisit with fresh attention.']::text[],quick_checks='[{"prompt":"A school claims its library extension improved evening access. Which evidence most strengthens the claim?","answer":"evening visits improved after the library extension, while the comparison conditions stayed stable.","explanation":"The correct choice directly measures evening access and includes a useful comparison."},{"prompt":"A school claims its waste signs improved proper sorting. Which evidence most strengthens the claim?","answer":"correctly sorted bins improved after the waste signs, while the comparison conditions stayed stable.","explanation":"The correct choice directly measures proper sorting and includes a useful comparison."},{"prompt":"A school claims its study workshop improved retention. Which evidence most strengthens the claim?","answer":"delayed recall scores improved after the study workshop, while the comparison conditions stayed stable.","explanation":"The correct choice directly measures retention and includes a useful comparison."}]'::jsonb,source_links='[{"title":"DepEd Strengthened Senior High School Program","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/strengthened-shs-program/"},{"title":"UPCAT official site","url":"https://upcat.up.edu.ph/"},{"title":"USTET official site","url":"https://ustet.ust.edu.ph/"}]'::jsonb,estimated_minutes=20,status='published' from public.topics t join public.subjects s on s.id=t.subject_id where l.topic_id=t.id and t.slug='arguments' and s.slug='mental-logical-reasoning';
update public.lessons l set overview='Judge whether given statements answer a question. This lesson moves from the underlying idea to a repeatable method, then applies it to CET-style examples. The goal is not just to remember an answer but to explain why the method works and when it applies.

Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element.',objectives=array['Identify a consistent relationship or pattern in data sufficiency.','Distinguish necessary conclusions from possible ones in data sufficiency.','Evaluate assumptions and evidence in data sufficiency.','Judge whether information is sufficient in data sufficiency.']::text[],concept_sections='[{"title":"Foundation","body":"Judge whether given statements answer a question."},{"title":"Governing rule","body":"Test each statement alone before combining them."},{"title":"CET application","body":"Reasoning items measure rule discovery and disciplined deduction. Separate the information given from assumptions, test the simplest consistent rule first, and require the rule to explain every element."}]'::jsonb,method_steps=array['List only the facts, positions, or symbols actually given.','Test simple features first: count, difference, ratio, order, direction, or category.','State the candidate rule explicitly.','Apply it to every element and reject it if even one element conflicts.','Use the surviving rule to derive the answer without adding unstated facts.']::text[],worked_examples='[{"title":"Guided example 1","passage":null,"prompt":"What is x? Statement 1: 2x + 3 = 19. Statement 2: x is positive. Which information is sufficient?","answer":"Statement 1 alone","solution":"Statement 1 uniquely gives x = (19 - 3)/2 = 8; positivity alone allows many values."},{"title":"Guided example 2","passage":null,"prompt":"What is x? Statement 1: 3x + 4 = 31. Statement 2: x is positive. Which information is sufficient?","answer":"Statement 1 alone","solution":"Statement 1 uniquely gives x = (31 - 4)/3 = 9; positivity alone allows many values."},{"title":"Guided example 3","passage":null,"prompt":"What is x? Statement 1: 4x + 5 = 45. Statement 2: x is positive. Which information is sufficient?","answer":"Statement 1 alone","solution":"Statement 1 uniquely gives x = (45 - 5)/4 = 10; positivity alone allows many values."}]'::jsonb,common_mistakes=array['Choosing a pattern that fits only the last two terms.','Reversing the direction of an analogy.','Assuming the converse of a conditional statement.','Combining statements before testing each one separately in data sufficiency.']::text[],exam_tips=array['Convert letters to positions and diagrams to simple feature lists.','For syllogisms, draw sets or use a small Venn diagram.','In arguments, ask which fact directly changes confidence in the conclusion.','Move on when no stable rule appears, then revisit with fresh attention.']::text[],quick_checks='[{"prompt":"What is x? Statement 1: 5x + 6 = 61. Statement 2: x is positive. Which information is sufficient?","answer":"Statement 1 alone","explanation":"Statement 1 uniquely gives x = (61 - 6)/5 = 11; positivity alone allows many values."},{"prompt":"What is x? Statement 1: 2x + 7 = 31. Statement 2: x is positive. Which information is sufficient?","answer":"Statement 1 alone","explanation":"Statement 1 uniquely gives x = (31 - 7)/2 = 12; positivity alone allows many values."},{"prompt":"What is x? Statement 1: 3x + 8 = 47. Statement 2: x is positive. Which information is sufficient?","answer":"Statement 1 alone","explanation":"Statement 1 uniquely gives x = (47 - 8)/3 = 13; positivity alone allows many values."}]'::jsonb,source_links='[{"title":"DepEd Strengthened Senior High School Program","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/strengthened-shs-program/"},{"title":"UPCAT official site","url":"https://upcat.up.edu.ph/"},{"title":"USTET official site","url":"https://ustet.ust.edu.ph/"}]'::jsonb,estimated_minutes=20,status='published' from public.topics t join public.subjects s on s.id=t.subject_id where l.topic_id=t.id and t.slug='data-sufficiency' and s.slug='mental-logical-reasoning';
commit;
